WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.670
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:09.870
You’re in for a real treat today, because
today, once again, we’re not going to simulate

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:12.340
just plain regular fluids.

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:13.340
No!

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:16.360
We’re going to simulate ferrofluids!

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:21.610
These are fluids that have magnetic properties
and respond to an external magnetic field

00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:25.450
and get this, they are even able even climb
things.

00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:27.410
Look at this footage from a previous paper.

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:33.870
Here is a legendary real experiment where
with magnetism, we can make a ferrofluid climb

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:36.239
up on this steel helix.

00:00:36.239 --> 00:00:37.579
Look at that.

00:00:37.579 --> 00:00:39.969
And now, the simulation.

00:00:39.969 --> 00:00:43.050
Look at how closely it matches the real footage.

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:49.069
Marvelous, especially that it is hard to overstate
how challenging it is to create an accurate

00:00:49.069 --> 00:00:51.940
simulation like this.

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:53.889
And the paper got even better.

00:00:53.889 --> 00:00:57.609
This footage could even be used as proper
teaching material.

00:00:57.609 --> 00:00:58.609
Look.

00:00:58.609 --> 00:01:04.250
On this axis, you can see how the fluid disturbances
get more pronounced as a response to a stronger

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:06.039
magnetic field.

00:01:06.039 --> 00:01:12.680
And in this direction, you see how the effect
of surface tension smooths out these shapes.

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:14.180
What a visualization!

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:19.920
The information density here is just out of
this world, while it is still so easy to read

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:21.310
at a glance.

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:24.600
And it is also absolutely beautiful.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:27.189
This paper was a true masterpiece.

00:01:27.189 --> 00:01:33.149
The first author of this work was Libo Huang
and it was advised by Prof. Dominik Michels

00:01:33.149 --> 00:01:35.509
who has a strong physics background.

00:01:35.509 --> 00:01:41.870
And here is the punchline: Libo Huang is a
PhD student and this was his first paper.

00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:48.229
Let me say it again: this was Libo Huang’s
first paper and it is a masterpiece.

00:01:48.229 --> 00:01:49.229
Wow!

00:01:49.229 --> 00:01:55.810
And it gets better, because this new paper
is called Surface-Only Ferrofluids…and YES!!!

00:01:55.810 --> 00:01:57.970
It is from the same authors.

00:01:57.970 --> 00:02:04.380
So this paper is supposed to be better, but
the previous technique set a really high bar.

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:06.369
How the heck do you beat that?!

00:02:06.369 --> 00:02:08.750
What more could we possibly ask for?

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:14.600
Well, this new method showcases a surface-only
formulation, and a key observation here that

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:19.550
for a class of ferrofluids, we don’t have
to compute how the magnetic forces act on

00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:24.990
the entirety of the 3D fluid domain, we only
have to compute them on the surface of the

00:02:24.990 --> 00:02:26.170
model.

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:28.660
So what does this give us?

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:30.870
One of my favorite experiments!

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:36.010
In this case, we squeeze the fluid between
two glass planes and start cranking up the

00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:39.620
magnetic field strength perpendicular to these
planes.

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:45.520
Of course, we expect that it starts flowing
sideways, but not at all how we would expect

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:46.520
it.

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:47.520
Wow.

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:53.120
Look as how these beautiful fluid labyrinths
start slowly forming.

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:57.060
And we can simulate all this on our home computers
today.

00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:01.140
We are truly living in a science fiction world.

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:06.260
Now if you find yourself missing the climbing
experiment from the previous paper, don’t

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:08.910
despair, this can still do that, look.

00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:15.160
First, we can control the movement of the
fluid by turning on the upper magnet, then,

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:21.240
slowly turn it off while turning on the lower
magnet to give rise to this beautiful climbing

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:22.240
phenomenon.

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:28.080
And that’s not all, fortunately, this work
is also ample in amazing visualizations, for

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:33.880
instance, this one shows how the ferrofluid
changes if we crank up the strength of our

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:39.160
magnets, and how changing the surface tension
determines the distance between the spikes

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:42.210
and the overall smoothness of the fluid.

00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:44.120
What a time to be alive!

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:48.620
One of the limitations of this technique is
that it does not deal with viscosity well,

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:54.130
so if we are looking to create a crazy goo
simulation like this one, but, with ferrofluids,

00:03:54.130 --> 00:03:56.730
we will need something else for that.

00:03:56.730 --> 00:04:40.380
Perhaps that something will be the next 

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:49.700
paper down the line.

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:53.680
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

